The one-wave idea did not work, so I'm sending it back as a recorded negative result. One wave of fat CTAs was never faster than the current one-shot grid on the test B200, and above about 200 MB it was 11–15% slower. The candidate below uses the one-wave path only for B·S ≤ 600, where it ties, and runs the parent's code everywhere else. It passed 16/16 workloads. Every size is within noise of the parent (S −0.2%, M −0.3%, L −0.3% on the bench). The emulator predicts 0.611 ± 0.009 against the current best 0.610, a coin flip. All probe timings come from the bench B200 at unlocked clocks, not the portal.

| Tokens (MB) | One-shot (parent) µs | One wave, 1184 CTAs, µs |
|---|---|---|
| 128 (12.6) | 4.16 | 4.19 |
| 256 (25) | 5.53 | 5.65 |
| 586 (58) | 9.15–9.40 | 9.21–9.25 |
| 1024 (101) | 14.46 | 14.47–14.82 |
| 2048 (201) | 27.4–28.1 | 30.9 |
| 4096 (403) | 57.6–57.8 | 64.8 |
| 8192 (805) | 114.2–115.7 | 131–132 |

- **No way to rescue the big sizes.** I also tried contiguous chunks per CTA, 2–4 rows in flight per thread, and prefetching the next 1–3 rows into registers. All were 8–48% slower at 805 MB; the best (prefetch one row ahead, 4 CTAs per SM) still took 123.8 µs. Fewer CTAs with deeper prefetch got worse quickly (170.6 µs at 2 CTAs per SM).
- **Probably no fixed cost left to remove at the small sizes.** At 128 tokens the parent's grid is already a single wave of 768 CTAs.
- **Plain stores, as a control:** dropping the evict_last hint on output stores cost +3.8% at 805 MB, +22% at 101 MB and +3% at 12.6 MB.
- **Probe tooling:** the timer sometimes fails with "No timing results for methodology: cupti"; a retry fixes it.

### Rationale
The one-shot grid already hides the cost of launching and retiring CTAs, so fat persistent CTAs only lose bandwidth once inputs pass the L2 size. The candidate keeps the one-wave path only where it ties, so a portal run would test whether it affects the S band on locked clocks.

